// Dev-server proxy for the `ng serve` inner loop: this surface running outside the
// docker-compose stack, on localhost:4200, against the composed API.
//
// It exists so AuthService's SIGN_IN_ENDPOINT can stay a relative path. The composed
// stack is already same-origin because nginx routes /v1/* to the API inside the network
// (infrastructure/local/web/nginx.conf); this is the same idea for the dev server. With
// both in place, neither way of running the app needs an API base URL reaching the
// bundle -- which is what CONVENTIONS.md's "Infrastructure impact" says to raise rather
// than bake in.
//
// The dev server reads this at serve time only (angular.json's `serve` target,
// `proxyConfig`). No module imports it and it reaches no build output.
//
// Comments are legal here: @angular/build parses a .json proxy config with jsonc-parser,
// not JSON.parse.
{
  // A plain prefix, not a glob. @angular/build passes a non-glob key through to Vite
  // untouched, and Vite proxies any path that starts with it -- so /v1/employees/sign-in
  // and its query string arrive at the API exactly as sent. A glob key would be
  // rewritten into a regex that has to match the query string too.
  //
  // There is deliberately no `pathRewrite`. Dropping the /v1 prefix would send every
  // call to the API root, which answers 404 -- and a 404 lands in AuthService's
  // `default:` branch as the generic failure message, which is the exact symptom this
  // proxy exists to remove. The composed stack hit the same class of problem on the
  // nginx side (LET-119).
  "/v1": {
    // 5080, not 8080: docker-compose publishes the API there because 8080 on the host is
    // already Keycloak's (infrastructure/local/docker-compose.yml, `api` ports).
    "target": "http://localhost:5080",

    // The local stack serves plain HTTP -- there is no certificate to validate.
    "secure": false
  }
}
